# Target C-II — the adjacent-strip KKT reaction is supported by at most two diameter bars except at one balanced antipodal degeneracy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only exact source-level structural theorem  
**Scope:** any adjacent O--O strip in the canonical full-support KKT normalization  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_adjacent_strip_opening_reduction_2026-09-15.md`; audited Section 157 cut resultants; C65 full actual-diameter visibility  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The adjacent-strip opening theorem gives an exact two-dimensional layer translation and the exact switching-force identity. The one/two-bar tangent-certificate theorem shows that only two moving diameter bars are needed to certify first-order translation infeasibility. The KKT identity gives a stronger **force-balanced** sparsification except in one explicit case.

Let `R` be the middle layer. Orient the two boundary contact families from the fixed side into `R`, with unit vectors

\[
a,b.
\]

Let their positive total KKT contact weights be

\[
A>0,
\qquad
B>0.
\]

For every switching actual diameter edge `h`, define the orientation-independent inward vector

\[
n_h=\delta_h r_h,
\qquad |n_h|=D,
\]

exactly as in the two-bar tangent theorem, and use one relative-interior normalized diameter stress

\[
\beta_h>0.
\]

Then the complete switching reaction is

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_h n_h
=Aa+Bb.
}
\tag{FR.1}
\]

Put

\[
G:=Aa+Bb.
\tag{FR.2}
\]

The theorem splits exactly according to whether `G` vanishes.

---

## Theorem FR

### Nondegenerate reaction

If

\[
G\ne0,
\]

there are one or two actual switching diameter edges

\[
J\subseteq E(H_{sw}),
\qquad
1\le|J|\le2,
\]

and positive coefficients `lambda_h` such that

\[
\boxed{
G=\sum_{h\in J}\lambda_h n_h.
}
\tag{FR.3}
\]

Thus the **exact KKT layer reaction**, not merely tangent infeasibility, has a one/two-diameter conic certificate.

### Balanced antipodal reaction

The equality

\[
G=0
\]

holds if and only if

\[
\boxed{
b=-a,
\qquad A=B.}
\tag{FR.4}
\]

In that case there is a positive dependence among at most three actual switching diameter vectors:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in J}\lambda_h n_h=0,
\qquad
2\le|J|\le3,
\qquad
\lambda_h>0.
}
\tag{FR.5}
\]

Three vectors can genuinely be necessary for a positive zero-sum dependence in the plane, so the number `3` in the degenerate branch must not be replaced by `2` without additional diameter-thrackle geometry.

The separate tangent-certificate theorem still gives a one/two-bar certificate in this balanced antipodal branch; what can require three bars is the **exact positive force balance**.

---

## 1. Recover the layer reaction with the physical strip orientations

Use the nested rooted shores from the laminar interval theorem. For the left boundary cut `tau_i`, the Section-157 train vector points from the complement of the far shore into the far shore. The middle layer lies on that far-shore side, so the physical outside-to-middle contact orientation is

\[
a=u_i.
\tag{FR.6}
\]

For the right boundary cut `tau_{i+1}`, the middle layer lies on the complement side of the farther nested shore. Hence its Section-157 orientation points **out of** the middle layer, and the physical outside-to-middle orientation is

\[
b=-u_{i+1}.
\tag{FR.7}
\]

Write the two exact cut resultants as

\[
F_i=A u_i,
\qquad
F_{i+1}=B u_{i+1}.
\tag{FR.8}
\]

The adjacent-strip theorem already proves

\[
F_i-F_{i+1}
=\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_h\delta_h r_h.
\tag{FR.9}
\]

Substituting (FR.6)--(FR.8) and `n_h=delta_h r_h` gives

\[
F_i-F_{i+1}
=Aa+Bb
=\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_hn_h,
\]

which is (FR.1).

No response inequality and no coefficient floor is used.

---

## 2. Conic Caratheodory gives two bars when the reaction is nonzero

Equation (FR.1) puts the vector `G` in the finitely generated cone

\[
G\in\operatorname{cone}\{n_h:h\in H_{sw}\}.
\tag{FR.10}
\]

In `R^2`, every **nonzero** vector in a finitely generated cone lies in the cone of at most two generators. One direct proof is to choose a representation of `G` using the fewest positive coefficients. If it used three or more nonzero generators, those planar generators would be linearly dependent. Moving the coefficient vector in a dependence direction until one positive coefficient becomes zero preserves the represented vector and contradicts minimality.

Therefore there are one or two actual switching edges and positive coefficients satisfying (FR.3).

Taking the dot product of (FR.3) with any tangent opening direction

\[
a\cdot w\ge0,
\qquad
b\cdot w\ge0
\]

gives

\[
\sum_{h\in J}\lambda_h(n_h\cdot w)
=G\cdot w
=A(a\cdot w)+B(b\cdot w)
\ge0.
\tag{FR.11}
\]

Hence at least one selected bar has `n_h dot w>=0`. Thus the force-balanced pair is automatically also a one/two-bar tangent obstruction certificate.

This recovers the previous Helly conclusion by a stronger equilibrium argument whenever `G!=0`.

---

## 3. The zero-reaction case is exactly balanced antipodality

Suppose

\[
Aa+Bb=0
\]

with `A,B>0` and `|a|=|b|=1`. Then

\[
Aa=-Bb.
\]

Taking norms gives

\[
A=B,
\]

and then

\[
b=-a.
\]

The converse is immediate. This proves (FR.4).

Equation (FR.1) now becomes a nontrivial positive dependence

\[
\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_hn_h=0,
\qquad
\beta_h>0.
\tag{FR.12}
\]

Normalize by the positive total switching multiplier. Then zero lies in the convex hull of the switching diameter vectors. Caratheodory's theorem in the plane gives a subset of at most three vectors whose convex hull contains zero. Taking an inclusion-minimal such subset makes all displayed coefficients positive.

A one-vector positive zero sum is impossible because every `n_h` has length `D>0`. Therefore the minimal subset has size two or three, proving (FR.5).

The three-vector possibility is real in abstract planar convex geometry: three non-antipodal vectors can positively surround the origin while no two are opposite. Eliminating it requires additional geometry of the actual diameter thrackle or the ordered middle-layer interface.

---

## 4. Exact finite frontier after the reaction sparsification

The current adjacent-strip line can now be split by one exact vector `G`.

### FR2 — nonzero reaction

```text
G = A a + B b != 0
+ G is the positive combination of one or two actual switching diameter vectors
+ the same one/two bars block every strict tangent-opening ray
+ if two bars are used, they are actual diameter-thrackle edges and therefore
  either cross properly or share an endpoint.
```

This is the generic branch.

### FR0 — balanced antipodal reaction

```text
b = -a
+ A = B
+ total switching KKT reaction is exactly zero
+ a positive force certificate needs at most three diameter bars
+ tangent infeasibility still has a separate one/two-bar certificate.
```

This is the only branch in which a genuinely triangular positive diameter-force balance can survive the planar sparsification.

The next useful geometry theorem should therefore attack either

1. the one/two-bar FR2 chord patterns using the current one-arc switching order; or
2. the exceptional FR0 three-bar pattern, where exact antipodality of the two boundary contact families and equality of the adjacent train-force magnitudes provide extra structure not present in the generic case.

---

## 5. Scope audit

- The theorem sparsifies the **reaction vector** in the two-dimensional layer-translation problem. It does not replace the global KKT stress by a one/two/three-edge stress.
- Other moving diameter edges remain exact active constraints and can obstruct a finite nonlinear translation even when they are absent from the sparse force certificate.
- In the zero-reaction branch, claiming a universal two-bar positive force balance would be false; three planar generators can be necessary.
- No KKT coefficient lower bound is used.
- No phase-gap or switching-count estimate is used.
- No exact triangle or source-level diameter decrease follows yet.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact sparse KKT reaction theorem for the adjacent-strip opening problem. C-II and Level I remain open.
